Z||000019||1FST2M6||11-19-2001||01:46 PM||||ben!! there is somthing SERIOUSLY wrong with you... your gunna make it go fast <B>AND</B> STOP TOO!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>rock on man!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> love your taste!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  you'll surely be a force to be reckoned with!||||reg||1
Z||000020||artherd||11-19-2001||01:54 PM||||Whups!  Forgot to post the offsets, that's the best part, they're *perfect* for our cars!!!<P>18x7.5"-50mm offset Front<BR>18x10"-65mm offset Rear<P>I'll need coilovers, in a BIG way.<BR>May even have to 'glass on' a set <BR>of slightly flared front fenders...<P>No way to know for sure untill everything is machined up and test-fit (AutoCAD gets me close, and says it should fit, but it's only as accurate as the measurment data I give it.  And that's not set in stone yet.)<P>I am counting on the 18" wheel size, as well as the (fairly small, belive it or not) 275 tyre on the 10" rim, to keep everything from contacting through the suspension travel range.<P>The 18x7.5-50" up front should be no problem, with a 225/40/18 tyre. (Grip hard, and still be fairly easy to turn.)<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Philphine:<BR><B>what kind of backspace do you think you'll need? think you can keep it in the wheel well? change to coilovers? tire size? i have some 17x10's and 18x10's (rears. 8.5" fronts for both) and hope to have one of them one one project. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000021||kevin||11-19-2001||02:50 PM||||Holy Cow! I will be focusing on your project as it develops. I want to know two things;do you belong to a Fiero club in your area? And, tell us which Porche these wheels/tires came off of? Once you get it completed, I expect numerous Fiero-istas in America will be calling Porche recycling yards. I am from No. Cal. and would hope to meet you at a Fiero club meeting.||||reg||14
Z||000022||Butter||11-19-2001||04:51 PM||||artherd<BR>NICE WHEELS!<P>Got a question for ya.<BR>In Tennessee when folks do that to their 4x4 trucks, there commonly refered to as a Rednecks.<P>What do they call California folks that do that to their cars?<P>It's Alright to be a Redneck.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif">    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif">    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif">   Kick it Leroy, Bubba's in the middle!<p>[This message has been edited by Butter (edited 11-19-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000023||artherd||11-19-2001||06:14 PM||||Hey there, I'm actually a member of a local club (CFOG, Classic Fiero Owners Group, and we're on the bay, get it? ;), and an officer of a new National(and Global/International) level club, CFOG-I (which, apart from sharing a similar name, and many members/officers from cfog, is a totally seperate, non-profit entity :)<P>We have some good times and help each other out.  And our local regional cfog/CA 'West Fest' meeting next year will be held in Lake Tahoe! (that's going to be fun :)<BR> <A HREF="http://www.cfog.org" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cfog.org</A>  (local club)  and <A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cfog-i.org</A>   (the International org!)<P>Thanks for the compliments, makes me feel a little less *CRAZY* about spending more than the car :S)     I'm trying to build a truely world-class sports car, on a honda budget, and I think it just might work when I'm done ;)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>Oh, PS: Wheels are off a brand new 2001 Porsche 911 Turbo. (the '996')  Brakes are off a 95-98 911 Twin Turbo (biturbo, Turbo. etc.)  the '993' eddition.  (they are larger than even the current 996's Turbo's brakes.)<P>More (much more :)  to come, once I get off my lazy arse and finish building the Northstar motor, work some more, machine some parts, buy a computer, yadda yadda yakity yak ;)<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by kevin:<BR><B>Holy Cow! I will be focusing on your project as it develops. I want to know two things;do you belong to a Fiero club in your area? And, tell us which Porche these wheels/tires came off of? Once you get it completed, I expect numerous Fiero-istas in America will be calling Porche recycling yards. I am from No. Cal. and would hope to meet you at a Fiero club meeting.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000024||Philphine||11-19-2001||09:37 PM||||ok. i'm not used to thinking in offsets. i always think back space. so if i did it right that means you have 7 9/16" backspace? i think both sets of wheels i have are right at 7" and i'm not sure if they'll miss the struts even with coilovers. i made me a little backspace calculating thingy (you know. just something to bolt to the mounting studs with a piece of allthread out at the point where i need the most back space) and it was so close i'm going to have to keep working at it (the 6000 swap i keep putting off) before i'll know. <P>i've got 275/40's on the 17's also mostly because it's a stock corvette size and i'm hoping if i go with them i can find deals on them ocassionaly. if the 18's work i have some 285/30's that came with the wheels that i would try. both are not much wider than the wheel itself so i'm hoping that will help fit them in.<P>good luck. if you get yours in maybe there's hope for me. <P>had to edit 'cause i just remembered to notice you have an '88 so you're working with different numbers. good luck anyway.<p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 11-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||Tina||11-19-2001||11:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR><B>I see a German trend there  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>He's right,...german wheels,german brakes...<P>Perhaps more than just the  "Silver thing"  rubbed off on you?<P><BR>HeHe<BR>Tina  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000026||frontal lobe||11-19-2001||11:06 PM||||If you are going to get all dressed up, then don't ruin it by putting on cheap shoes!<P>NICE wheels.  For that price, I hope they threw in the air for free!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Or do they have to fill it with some inert gas??  Xenon?||||reg||1
Z||000027||artherd||11-20-2001||12:22 AM||||"That thing's so exotic, the tyres probally require imported air!" -some magazine describing the McLaren 'F1'.<P>Erk.  Well. Not Xenon.  Nitrogen :)  Not required, but I've got a cylinder of it, so my tyres get N2 :)  I must be insane. Is that blue bottle leaking?  You look funny!!!<P>XDrewX: Haven't decided on body mods really...   Except... this one will eventually be a cabriolet (that's french for 'convertible'  Know what else means convertible? a lot!) Spyder, Spider, Roadster, Barchetta, etc.    Not in the first stage, and not untill I can make the X-frame really really strong. (and the top stable at speed.)<P>Trav: Couldn't let you be the only one here running 12s with 18s :)  Yeah, I shot fire out of the stock brakes (on an 88, with a stock motor) coming down from 120 the other day. The idea of 150+ (that's a big plus too) speed capability scared me into the Big Reds :)  I mean, seeing as I plan to work the heads/lifters for around 400hp later... (and I've got a friend who really wants to turbo the bejesus out of a N* one day...  that'll probally need the tripple plate clutch, double plate is only rated to hold ~900lb-ft :0)<P>Hey, you think I'm crazy?  Wow!  That's probally the highest compliment I've ever recieved! ;)  Hope to get our cars properly introduced one of these days, you can drive mine if I can drive yours! (PS: how's the 'mystery intake/fogger' setup coming?!)<P>Tina: Who's obbsessed?  Just wait till you see my engine compartment/firewall!<P>"There's something about those silver ones"<P>Butter: Hrm, there are names, but if I get it all done right they'll be refering to me as "that red streak" (the silver dopler-shifted to IR and red bands :)   YOU MIGHT be a redneck if you stuff porsche rims on your car! ;)<P>Philphine:  Hrm... I think that backspacing calculation is correct :) I'm so used to thiniing in terms of center offset, heh!<P>Your wheels sound like Z06 C5 stock, coool ;)<P>Yeah, the 88 numbers are a little different, and since I am re-working the suspension/hubs at a pretty low-level, I will have some relocation lattitude...  I've about guestimated/measured myself silly,  Right now it's all too close to tell untill they actually get here.   That will again be a day for (good, 35mm, promise) pics, I assure you all :)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>Oh! there is a company who actually adapts these awesome brakes to 84-87s, but not 88s. (and front only I belive) <A HREF="http://www.movit.de/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.movit.de/</A>   They have a US distributor; <A HREF="http://www.ultimategarage.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ultimategarage.com/</A>  <P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by frontal lobe:<BR><B>If you are going to get all dressed up, then don't ruin it by putting on cheap shoes!<P>NICE wheels.  For that price, I hope they threw in the air for free!   :)  Or do they have to fill it with some inert gas??  Xenon?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000028||Flamberge||11-20-2001||04:54 AM||||Artherd: NICE wheels... however, I like your old ones better (the ones on your red car.) In fact, the only other rims I have seen on a Fiero that I like as much as your "double spoke" rims are the ones on FieroLisa's car, which might be the same rims, since they are similar. <P>You have told us what the dimensions/offset of the new rims are, so what are the old rims? I would love a set!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Thanks in advance, man!<BR><P>------------------<BR>- Flamberge<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://flamberge.iwarp.com/Serra3sm.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000029||artherd||12-06-2001||03:37 AM||||They're here! They're here! They're here!<P>Oh my GOD they're friggin HUGE man!!<P>Too dark for pics, and all I'd do is lay them in the wheel wells anyway (need hubs machined now.) Hey, neat, they sorta/almost fit in the rear. kinda.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>PS: thanks for the compliments, the 'old' 17s on the T-top car (which I'm keeping! :0) are Enki Phalanex (and aparently fairly rare, hope I never dent/ding one!) Anyway, Lisa's wheels are different, though in pics at a distance they look really similar!<P>I've kind of fallen for the whole 10 or double-5 spoke wheel thing withought realising it.  My *absolute* favourite wheels in the world are either the 512TR's or the F355's 5-spokes.  <P>But these 996 BBS wheels aren't too shabby!<P>Anyway, I'm like a kid in a candy store here, this has all been a LONG time in coming (been saving for several years now) I keep going out to the garage to make sure the wheels and the N* are still there, it's all coming together so well, this is gonna be great   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">     <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red                     "Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                                        -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"<p>[This message has been edited by artherd (edited 12-06-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000030||reddog||12-07-2001||08:09 PM||||Very nice!!!!<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Picturenew_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000031||88-DOHC||12-07-2001||10:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>18x7.5"-50mm offset Front<BR>18x10"-65mm offset Rear<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Fronts will tuck under nicely, rears may actually tuck under too far with that offset and width.<P>The wheels I put on mine, the fronts where 50mm offest and rears were 35mm, just perfect for the 88.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>BTW, the friend I had bought my car from actually had done the conversion to Porsche rims, you might want to see what he did on his webpage (if you havn't seen it already).<P><A HREF="http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/Boxster/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/Boxster/index.html</A><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000032||Nashco||12-07-2001||11:51 PM||||88-DOHC:  Those measurements are almost spot-on of what is desired.  The fronts should be perfect and the rears will stick OUT further than the stock wheels.  Remember, offset is affected by the wheel width too.  Did you use a 10" rear wheel?  I'm doubting it <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I can't wait to see what that stuff looks like bolted up...AT LEAST just stuck in there as a motivation decoy <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000033||88-DOHC||12-08-2001||12:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Nashco:<BR><B>88-DOHC:  Those measurements are almost spot-on of what is desired.  The fronts should be perfect and the rears will stick OUT further than the stock wheels.  Remember, offset is affected by the wheel width too.  Did you use a 10" rear wheel?  I'm doubting it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I can't wait to see what that stuff looks like bolted up...AT LEAST just stuck in there as a motivation decoy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>No, I used 18"x8" wheels (in the rear) so based on that, (his wheels) will tuck in slightly more then mine. So yes, you are right.I had forgotten about the widths, and taking that into account. Silly me. <P>Yes, his wheels stick out a bit more then stock (about 1/2"), but overall probably will look the same (depth wise, in relation to the car) due to these being 18" wheels and will have very little to no side wall bulge.||||reg||1
Z||000034||artherd||12-08-2001||02:14 AM||||Hi Guys!<P>88DOHC, you have that car? Awesome!  His old site is actually what got the idea cooking in my head so long ago!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>PS: I think you mean 50mm front, and 55mm rear, right?<P>PPS: yes, since my wheels are from a 996, they're 10" (fronts are the same as your boxster rims at 7.5" though!)<P>You could probally mount up 996 rears if you so desired (though I bet your 8.5s stick nicely!)<P>Yeah, I get a bonous with the width, they'll stick OUT about .5" more than normal, which is juuust about right, while they'll stick IN  ~2.5"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I'm barely going to get away with it by doing several tricks, first I'll actually have about +- .5" of design lattitude with the new suspension and hubs being machined, second, Held rear coilovers and the height of 18", and lastly, the 265 tyre  on a 10" wheel is actually bowed IN about 30 degrees. (tyre is almost too small for the rim, makes it cantilevered in a tad, so I get a little more travel before any contact, hopefully enough!)<P>Phew!  Pics dummyed up tommorow, promise!<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88-DOHC:<BR><B> <BR>Fronts will tuck under nicely, rears may actually tuck under too far with that offset and width.<P>The wheels I put on mine, the fronts where 50mm offest and rears were 35mm, just perfect for the 88.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>BTW, the friend I had bought my car from actually had done the conversion to Porsche rims, you might want to see what he did on his webpage (if you havn't seen it already).<P><A HREF="http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/Boxster/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/Boxster/index.html</A></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000035||88-DOHC||12-08-2001||05:20 AM||||I didn't get the porsche rims, his wifes car inherited those. Besides I wanted my coil-overs, drop spindles and 13" brake package I had bought for my previous car on this car. So we played musical parts between 3 cars. Because of the brakes I had to purchase some wheels, so I ended up with some fairly custom wheels, fronts are 18"x7"x50mm offset, rears are 18"x8"x35mm. Mine hardly even stick out even though my rear is an additional 3/4" greater width then stock. So based on my calculations your wheels will actually be about 14mm further in then mine. I don't think you have to worry about your wheels sticking out of the fender wheels.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>Hi Guys!<P>88DOHC, you have that car? Awesome!  His old site is actually what got the idea cooking in my head so long ago!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>PS: I think you mean 50mm front, and 55mm rear, right?<P>PPS: yes, since my wheels are from a 996, they're 10" (fronts are the same as your boxster rims at 7.5" though!)<P>You could probally mount up 996 rears if you so desired (though I bet your 8.5s stick nicely!)<P>Yeah, I get a bonous with the width, they'll stick OUT about .5" more than normal, which is juuust about right, while they'll stick IN  ~2.5"   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I'm barely going to get away with it by doing several tricks, first I'll actually have about +- .5" of design lattitude with the new suspension and hubs being machined, second, Held rear coilovers and the height of 18", and lastly, the 265 tyre  on a 10" wheel is actually bowed IN about 30 degrees. (tyre is almost too small for the rim, makes it cantilevered in a tad, so I get a little more travel before any contact, hopefully enough!)<P>Phew!  Pics dummyed up tommorow, promise!<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P><BR> <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
